Limit: a release ends the binding and does not stop work the released runtime has in flight; whether that makes the write unsafe is BindingRegistry.revoke's question and is asked at the write, not at this authorization
Ruled-out: one replay map holding both doors' receipts | the stored receipt type would be a union no call site can narrow, and a requestId reused across the two doors carries a different digest either way, so neither door can replay the other's answer without it
Ruled-out: re-keying the partial-timestamp-orderings entry for agentcpd.ts a fifth time | four changes in a row went red on it and none touched the query; the ordering was given handoff_id instead, which is the remedy that file's header prescribes, and the entry is gone rather than relocated
Ruled-out: a second copy of the MCP internal-denial sanitization for the release tool | it would be a second authority on one fact and would leave delegated-mcp-internal-denial-is-closed's anchor matching twice, so both tools publish through one helper instead
Warn: releasing is deliberately not conditioned on the incumbent being proven dead, which rebinding requires: a replacement must prove the incumbent gone because two live runtimes would both believe they hold the role, while a release leaves the role held by nobody, so a live incumbent is the ordinary case at this door rather than the dangerous one
Warn: the socket fixture's target used one constant attestationDigest while both production targets digest the verified tuple, and actor_target_attestations_no_replace aborts on a repeated (target_binding_id, attestation_digest); no case had ever rebound the same session, so that shortcut read as a control-plane rule until a release made rebinding one session ordinary
